WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
NARRATOR: If European imports
like horses and cattle

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.366
forever changed the Americas,

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:10.033
American exports to Europe would
forever change life there.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.166
The humble potato is
one important example.

00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:17.800
It was grown by peoples of the
Andes for hundreds of years

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.601
before the Spaniards arrived.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:21.433
Once in Europe,

00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:24.901
this nutritious vegetable
became the food of the poor,

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:26.532
and it supported a sharp increase

00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:30.333
in the European population
beginning in the 1600s.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.866
Ireland’s population grew
more than eightfold.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:38.499
For many, meals consisted of
potatoes and nothing else.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.434
But then another import
from the Americas arrived:

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:44.368
a deadly fungus.

00:00:44.368 --> 00:00:47.066
By the summer of 1845,

00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:51.567
potato fields stricken by this
fungus blackened overnight.

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:55.300
The Irish potato famine had begun.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:01.001
Those who had lived by the
potato now died without it.

00:01:01.001 --> 00:01:04.000
Perhaps a million Irish starved to death.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.467
A million others took to
ships headed westward

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.333
across the Atlantic.

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.734
They became part of perhaps
the most significant cargo

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:14.466
to move between the continents:

00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:18.500
the massive wave of people
who would cross the oceans

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.601
to start a new life in the Americas.
